{
    "word": "arboreous",
    "lang_code": "en",
    "source_file_to_entries": {
        "raw-wiktextract-data.jsonl": [
            {
                "entry_id": "9dfffcb1-20cf-3182-aa11-ea998daeac1a",
                "word": "arboreous",
                "pos": "adj",
                "lang_code": "en",
                "forms": [],
                "senses": [
                    {
                        "sense_id": "043f5613-5ff1-3ee9-b91c-80238726a349",
                        "entry_id": "9dfffcb1-20cf-3182-aa11-ea998daeac1a",
                        "glosses": [
                            "Having the characteristics of a tree. (of a plant)"
                        ],
                        "tags": [],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "fee21c19-ed73-47f8-8659-760521ca7983",
                                "sense_id": "043f5613-5ff1-3ee9-b91c-80238726a349",
                                "text": "1684, Thomas Browne, “Observations upon Several Plants Mention’d in Scripture” in Certain Miscellany Tracts, London: Charles Mearne, pp. 28-29,\nFor the Parable may not […] imply any or every grain of Mustard, but point at such a grain as from its fertile spirit, and other concurrent advantages, hath the success to become arboreous, shoot into such a magnitude, and acquire the like tallness."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "2465dd46-53e2-4776-8e4e-712441a95992",
                                "sense_id": "043f5613-5ff1-3ee9-b91c-80238726a349",
                                "text": "[…] the continental climate, that is, having a colder winter and warmer summer, capable of producing considerable vigour of arboreous vegetation, and not so favourable to the generating of […] peat-moss […]"
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "99424cf7-266b-4ded-8a84-4effe6efd8c1",
                                "sense_id": "043f5613-5ff1-3ee9-b91c-80238726a349",
                                "text": "1918, Edgar Rice Burroughs, Out of Time’s Abyss in The Complete Works of Edgar Rice Burroughs, Hastings, UK: Delphi Classics, 2014, Chapter 1,\ndense forests of eucalyptus and acacia and giant arboreous ferns with feathered fronds waving gently a hundred feet above their heads"
                            }
                        ]
                    },
                    {
                        "sense_id": "cfa21b14-8afd-320b-9941-478c5663050d",
                        "entry_id": "9dfffcb1-20cf-3182-aa11-ea998daeac1a",
                        "glosses": [
                            "Covered or filled with trees."
                        ],
                        "tags": [],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "55cb543a-b878-483e-aeee-1c167b2122d9",
                                "sense_id": "cfa21b14-8afd-320b-9941-478c5663050d",
                                "text": "The country was no longer plain-land, but an arboreous wilderness interspersed with small fields and fruit orchards."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "378f2c6f-9fb6-4cb4-b940-dfd517871307",
                                "sense_id": "cfa21b14-8afd-320b-9941-478c5663050d",
                                "text": "But among Authours, we meet with nothing more frequent, and indeed more celebrated, than those Arboreous amenities and Plantations of Woods, which they call’d Luci;"
                            }
                        ]
                    },
                    {
                        "sense_id": "b4d6fc89-50d4-39a8-b69d-f5de039e66e4",
                        "entry_id": "9dfffcb1-20cf-3182-aa11-ea998daeac1a",
                        "glosses": [
                            "Growing on trees."
                        ],
                        "tags": [
                            "obsolete"
                        ],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "ba9195c2-9eea-4b11-9f81-6ed77969b16f",
                                "sense_id": "b4d6fc89-50d4-39a8-b69d-f5de039e66e4",
                                "text": "And those fruites whiche Galene calleth arboreous, are those growing vppon trees."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "3370c59a-7725-47fc-bd14-ff9843159179",
                                "sense_id": "b4d6fc89-50d4-39a8-b69d-f5de039e66e4",
                                "text": "Mushromes are either terrestrial, which grow out of the earth, or arboreous, which adhere to the stocks of trees;"
                            }
                        ]
                    },
                    {
                        "sense_id": "96e0ee5d-eb12-3746-95b6-7c72030b26d7",
                        "entry_id": "9dfffcb1-20cf-3182-aa11-ea998daeac1a",
                        "glosses": [
                            "Having a tree-like, branching structure."
                        ],
                        "tags": [
                            "obsolete"
                        ],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "f54138a3-af82-4951-a5fc-e362232e46ea",
                                "sense_id": "96e0ee5d-eb12-3746-95b6-7c72030b26d7",
                                "text": "The arboreous ramification of the Meditallium of the Cerebellum appearing, being cut right downwards."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "073b3bf5-0c1a-4133-81e1-476ac90b34d9",
                                "sense_id": "96e0ee5d-eb12-3746-95b6-7c72030b26d7",
                                "text": "1698, William Cowper, The Anatomy of Humane Bodies, Oxford: S. Smith and B. Walford, Table 56,\n[…] [the] Internal Concave Surface [of the Placenta Uterina] next the Amnios, Appears Cover’d with the Chorion; under which the Arboreous Disposition of its Blood-Vessels are elegantly Exprest."
                            }
                        ]
                    }
                ],
